tess (cust-success): Hey team, enterprise customer on Dedicated reports intermittent SSE sessions that stream partial output then drop with socket errors on the server. They see spikes in billing because clients reconnect and re-request the same stream. Rolling this to support to triage — attaching their minimal repro and server logs below.

alan (support): Hi Tess — can you paste the exact log lines and the request id? also which model/route and whether they use our hosted gateway or their private deploy?

tess (cust-success): Request id x-req=R-9a3b2c, model redwood-lite-1. They are on Dedicated in us-west-2 behind an ALB (customer-managed). Sample server log (trimmed):

```
2026-02-28T16:12:03Z [runtime] write error: write EAGAIN (errno=11) on fd 58
2026-02-28T16:12:03Z [runtime] aborting stream R-9a3b2c: backpressure exceeded
2026-02-28T16:12:04Z [auth] token refresh failed: 401 invalid_token
2026-02-28T16:12:04Z [billing] stream-session new charge id=B-44a
```

They also see 502s at the ALB occasionally and clients report seeing the last chunk truncated.

marco (eng-runtime): thanks — sounds like classic slow-consumer backpressure causing the serving process to hit its send loop limits and then abort. a few quick questions: 1) are they using our SSE text/event-stream endpoint (Transfer-Encoding: chunked)? 2) do they have any reverse proxies in front with buffering (nginx/proxy buffering on)? 3) what's their socket send buffer size / backlog?

alan (support): customer replies: yes SSE endpoint, nginx in front with `proxy_buffering on` and ALB idle timeout 60s. client is a JS single-page app that occasionally goes background/tab-suspended and stops reading.

sasha (sre): if the client stops reading, kernel backpressure will cause the send(2) to return EAGAIN or block. We should avoid blocking the worker thread. We have two failure modes: a) the server keeps buffering until system limits then returns ENOBUFS/EAGAIN and we abort, b) the edge proxy buffers and then silently drops long-held connections resulting in partial output to client.

marco (eng-runtime): repro steps we used locally — helps reproduce fast:

Server (Node-ish pseudo) that streams fast:
```
http.createServer((req,res)=>{
  res.writeHead(200,{'Content-Type':'text/event-stream'});
  for(let i=0;i<10000;i++){
    if(!res.write(`data: chunk ${i}\n\n`)){ // simulate kernel backpressure
      // don't block; log and drop after threshold
      console.error('write returned false',i);
    }
  }
});
```

Client (slow reader)
```
const http=require('http');
http.get(url,res=>{
  res.on('data',d=>{ setTimeout(()=>{},2000); /* block processing */ });
});
```

When client delays processing, send buffer fills -> `res.write` returns false on Node. If the runtime doesn't handle that properly and polls on `write` again synchronously, file descriptors stall and we see EAGAIN/EPIPE logs.

marco (eng-runtime): concrete suggestions / mitigations:
- Ensure fronting proxies have `proxy_buffering off` and `send_timeout` tuned. ALB: increase idle timeout > client's expected max pause.
- Use small chunk sizes and frequent SSE keepalive pings (`: ping` comments) so TCP segments flush and don't accumulate large buffers.
- On server, avoid spinning on blocked writes. Respect `write()` backpressure signals (write returns false) and queue outgoing events in an async queue with a per-stream max queue length and drop/trim older frames when queue exceeds threshold.
- Consider disabling Nagle (TCP_NODELAY) on socket for small writes if appropriate.
- Tune kernel knobs: `net.core.wmem_max`, `net.ipv4.tcp_wmem`, `fs.file-max`, `net.core.somaxconn` for high connection counts.

alan (support): customer asks: why are they seeing a 401 in logs right after the write errors? is that related?

marco (eng-runtime): likely because the client reconnect flow uses a short-lived ephemeral token and the client process that was backgrounded forgot to refresh the token before reconnecting, so reconnect attempt hits 401 and we record a failed auth; then client retries with a fresh token and that creates a duplicate stream start -> double billing. Recommend adding idempotency key on stream start or server-side dedup by session id.

sasha (sre): one more operational note: if the process aborts the worker to recover (OOM or stuck fd), load balancer might return 502. Also check for TCP RST vs FIN in connection trace. If it's RST from upstream, that means process killed/abort. If FIN, graceful close.

